Surprisingly, the classifiers with the least accuracy drop is Bernoulli Naive Bayes with accuracy difference of only 0.093 and K-Nearest
Neighbor with accuracy difference of only 0.095. 

The classifiers with the highest accuracy drop are interestingly Random Forest (0.55) and LightBoost (0.56).

Completely contradicting my expectation, the ensemble and boosters underperformed whereas a Naive Bayes outperformed them.



In the previous case, the classifiers were tested at a single noise level. This didn't bring justice to ensemble and boosters. 
Therefore, they will be re-tested again using four differen noise levels.
Lower noise levels : 2% and 5%; they have only 2 to 7 of cells flipped in a row full of 131 cells.
Higher noise levels : 10% and 15%; they have about 13 to 20 cells flipped per row.
The lower noise levels also reflect the real world patient forgetting to report some symptoms.

import numpy as np
import pandas as pd

def corrupt(df, flip_frac=0.15, seed=42):
    rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)
    mask = rng.random(df.shape) < flip_frac
    return df ^ mask.astype(int)

results = []
for frac in [0.02, 0.05, 0.10, 0.15]:
    X_test_noisy = corrupt(wow, flip_frac=frac)
    for name, model in classifier_dictionary.items():
        acc = accuracy_score(encoded_symbi_disease, model.predict(X_test_noisy))
        results.append({"flip_frac": frac, "model": name, "accuracy": round(acc, 4)})

results_df = pd.DataFrame(results)
pivot = results_df.pivot(index="model", columns="flip_frac", values="accuracy")
print(pivot)

