Observation : Be it at the lowest noise level (2%) or highest noise level (15%), Bernoulli and KNN didn't drop their accuracy below 90%.
But LightBoost started getting worse at only 5% noise level.

Conclusion : The ensemble and booster never learned the symptoms -> disease relationships, they just memorized the training patterns. 
They overfitted hard for this specific dataset whereas the others generalized.


The Bernoulli and KNN might "win" in this specific synthetic dataset but if I have to push it into production and deploy it to real-world
end users, maybe the ensembles will win this time as they are literally built to handle the complex real world data?

The answer is No. The ensembles will win in handling real-world patient data if and if only they were trained on such messy, unseen data.
The corruption test is a legitimate way of seeing which machine would tolerate real world messiness better.
The ensembles showed their competence by breaking down at even a slight unfamiliarity from the symptoms they had memorized.
But, Bernoulli/KNN/LogReg consistently proved to be able to handle any level of noise.

